# core v3.7 — NEGATIVE. An uppercase hex digest.
#
# THIS IS NOT A CONSTRAINT ABOUT TASTE, AND THE VALUE HERE IS THE SAME DIGEST AS
# THE VALID FIXTURE'S, CASED DIFFERENTLY. That is the point: the two strings
# denote the identical bytes, and if both spellings were accepted the store
# would hold one document under two names. Cross-source deduplication and
# idempotent re-import both follow from the file's name BEING its digest, and
# both stop working the moment one digest has two spellings.
#
# The second half is the filesystem. This string is a FILENAME, and a
# case-insensitive filesystem -- which is the default on macOS, where this
# project's reference implementations run -- will happily open the lowercase
# file when asked for the uppercase one, so the two spellings collide on one
# machine and do not on another. A defect that depends on which laptop ran the
# import is worse than one that always happens.
#
# The same clause is why base64 is not accepted either: its alphabet contains
# "/", which would fabricate a path separator inside a filename, and it is
# case-sensitive in a place where the filesystem may not be.
#
# The path is left lowercase and well formed, so the digest property is the only
# thing rejected and the fixture cannot pass for the wrong reason. Rejected by
# cascade:AttachmentShape / cascade:contentHash / sh:pattern.
#
# Fully synthetic apart from the digest, which is a real hash of a synthetic
# string.

@prefix cascade: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/core/v1#> .

<urn:uuid:a77a0307-0000-4000-8000-0000000000a7> a cascade:Attachment ;
    cascade:attachmentPath
        "attachments/sha-256/8dd3c6b5f593b25cb9dc0094d67323d16c3bbc9584eda019726a38dd2cc7a471" ;
    cascade:contentHash
        "8DD3C6B5F593B25CB9DC0094D67323D16C3BBC9584EDA019726A38DD2CC7A471" ;
    cascade:hashAlgorithm "sha-256" ;
    cascade:attachmentMediaType "application/pdf" .
